WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.040
AI FARMS:
AI is ruining YouTube.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:07.060
Gone are the days of genuine creators, pouring
their hearts into their videos, sharing their

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:10.670
vulnerabilities, and forming a deep connection
with their audience.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:16.560
I’m not talking about cases in which creators
us AI to make better content.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:28.080
I’m talking about videos like this and this
and this, content that’s completely generated

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.690
using AI from beginning to end.

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:37.110
Of course, this is most common within the
video essay space but all niches from sports

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.480
compilations and TV show predictions to tech
news and financial news have seen a rise in

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.820
AI generated content.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:50.690
On one hand, it’s insane that such content
can be created completely using AI.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:55.050
Everything from the idea generation, script
writing, and pacing to the voiceover, editing,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.780
and thumbnail.

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:02.940
But on the other hand, this completely defeats
the entire purpose of YouTube.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:08.009
The core to YouTube’s success was not about
high-budget productions like MrBeast or a

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:14.000
bunch of music videos from popular singers
or flashy displays, it was always the authenticity

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.800
that stirred millions of hearts and minds.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.390
From heart wrenching geometry dash meltdowns
that are too relatable to creators opening

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:25.170
up and sharing their insecurities and worst
moments.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:30.799
This has always been the magic of not just
YouTube but all forms of media, even traditional

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:31.909
TV.

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:37.560
How many people watch something just because
they love a specific character or hate a specific

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.560
character.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:45.250
These stories and emotions filled with creativity,
diversity, and genuine human connection are

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:49.510
what make visual communication so engaging,
so compelling.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:54.490
These AI farms of course try to replicate
this by telling ChatGPT to write stories in

00:01:54.490 --> 00:02:00.060
a three act structure or by discussing big
well known people but you can usually immediately

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.310
tell when a video is made using AI, atleast
so far.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.990
Sometimes, it’s as obvious as the voice
being a robot.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:12.599
Othertimes, it’s a much more subtle feeling
that everything that you’re watching is

00:02:12.599 --> 00:02:15.849
the exact same thing, like a broken record.

00:02:15.849 --> 00:02:20.819
These automation channels have become masters
of clickbait and manipulating the algorithm,

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:23.970
saturating our feeds with low-quality content.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.220
They found a formula, and they stick to it
relentlessly.

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:33.599
You’ll sometimes even see videos with the
exact same titles and thumbnails, the same

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:37.380
recycled footage, and even the same pacing
and story.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:41.800
But whatever gives it away, it’s the same
distant feeling.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:45.980
Is this why 2.5 billion people decided to
watch YouTube?

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.230
To see robots recap random movies that we’ve
never heard of?

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.909
Or was it to experience something?

00:02:52.909 --> 00:03:04.659
The high of conquering an impossible level,
the wonder of traveling, and the pure joy

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:08.230
of passionately uniting around a niche topic.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:12.680
These are the sparks that ignited YouTube’s
flame in the first place, bringing together

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:18.379
billions of people worldwide and it’s these
same sparks that AI is extinguishing from

00:03:18.379 --> 00:03:19.750
the platform.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:23.610
But there’s an even darker side to AI content.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.140
Like with any gold rush, the people who are
actually profiting are the ones selling the

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.140
shovels as thousands of creators are being
sold the idea that they too can make a million

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:35.569
dollars a year posting such content.

00:03:35.569 --> 00:03:40.970
So, join me as we uncover the dark side of
AI-generated content and its impact on the

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:48.300
authenticity that once defined this platform.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:57.810
TOP 10 VIDEOS:
Completely AI generated content is very much

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:58.810
a new phenomenon.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:02.530
ChatGPT hasn’t even been around for a year
at this point.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:08.760
But unlike AI content itself, the distant
feeling associated with this content isn’t

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:09.760
new.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.769
This distant feeling can attributed to YouTube
automation which can actually be dated back

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:20.269
to almost the beginning of YouTube itself
to January 25, 2007.

00:04:20.269 --> 00:04:28.520
This was the day that one of the most iconic
channels of all time was created: WatchMojo.com.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:34.580
These guys are now known as the OG content
farm but they didn’t start off that way.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:40.120
In fact, for the first many years, they were
just posting simple tutorials and streetside

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.580
interviews.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:48.010
Some of these videos did get tens of thousands
of views but most didn’t do so great.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:53.970
In fact, many of them haven’t even crossed
the thousand view mark after 15 years.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:59.729
Clearly, this wasn’t working, so WatchMojo
shifted to making translation content.

00:04:59.729 --> 00:05:04.860
We’re talking Japanese translations, Hebrew
translations, Arabic translations, Russian

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.870
translations, and then there was the random
yoga videos as well.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:18.330
These videos got much more consistent views
but it wasn’t till September 18, 2010 that

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:24.060
WatchMojo really cracked the code with the
video: top 10 Julia Roberts Movies.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:30.840
This got just under a million views but then
WatchMojo went back to their classic content.

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.979
Fall fashion from in wear, 332 views.

00:05:33.979 --> 00:05:36.810
Fashion Show: fall trends, 245 views.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.919
But then they posted, Top 10 Will Smith Movies,
2.7 million views.

00:05:40.919 --> 00:05:49.760
And then, they did it again, and again, and
again, and again, and again.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.680
Each and every one of these videos were pulling
in hundreds of thousand if not millions of

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:55.680
views.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.789
And before you knew it, WatchMojo would become
a full on top 10 channel putting out thousands

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:05.639
upon thousands of these videos, dozens of
which garnered tens of millions of views.

00:06:05.639 --> 00:06:11.270
I was one of these hundreds of millions of
viewers back in the day but looking back,

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:15.430
I couldn’t tell you what exactly made these
videos so popular.

00:06:15.430 --> 00:06:19.840
Many of these lists wouldn’t even take an
hour to compile and all of the footage you

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.889
needed was already out there.

00:06:21.889 --> 00:06:27.720
So, there was nothing particularly special
about these videos but at the same time, there

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.130
clearly was.

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:34.370
Maybe, it was fans of Will Smith and Angelina
Jolie and all of these other topics wanting

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:39.310
to congregate and reminisce about their favorite
actor or singers’ best and worst moments.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:42.259
Or maybe, it was just plain curiosity.

00:06:42.259 --> 00:06:45.539
What actually are Bruce Lee’s top 10 moments?

00:06:45.539 --> 00:06:49.340
Whatever the reason though, these videos were
some of the most popular in the world back

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.280
in the early and mid 2010s.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.960
As such, we saw dozens and dozens of copycat
channels pop up like TheRichest, MostAmazingTop10,

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:01.880
Looper, TopTrending, Alux.com, Mr. Luxury,
and so many more.

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:05.520
All of them were basically just content farms.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:10.400
They would outsource the entire video production
process from scripting and voiceovers to editing

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:11.500
and thumbnail design.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:18.080
They didn’t have the help of AI back in
the day but these videos were basically indiscernible

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.010
from modern AI videos.

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:24.850
The goal wasn’t to tell a story or to convey
a message or to make a change.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:30.360
The goal was to just create mindless entertainment
by focusing on quantity, quantity, quantity.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:36.199
2, 3, 4, 5, uploads every single day, an upload
every few hours.

00:07:36.199 --> 00:07:42.539
Most of these videos would do alright, some
would do bad, but a select few, these would

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:45.949
go viral making the entire operation worth
it.

00:07:45.949 --> 00:07:51.729
But, almost as quickly as these channels rose
to fame, they fell off a cliff.

00:07:51.729 --> 00:07:57.370
Taking a look at WatchMojo today, while they
do have 25 million subscribers, their recent

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.970
videos don’t get anymore views than their
2008 and 9 videos.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:05.319
It’s even worse with TheRichest as many
of their videos struggle to even cross the

00:08:05.319 --> 00:08:09.030
10k mark despite having 15 million subscribers.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:11.930
Some of these channels have even stopped posting
altogether.

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.470
It was official.

00:08:13.470 --> 00:08:18.580
The days of milking top 10 videos had come
to an end but this was just in time for a

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:23.639
new style of automation to take off.

00:08:23.639 --> 00:08:33.760
ANIMATED VIDEOS:
On July 9, 2013, over 6 years after WatchMojo

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:40.930
was created, we would see a new channel enter
the space with a rather hard name: Kurzgesagt.

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:43.830
Kurzgesagt was by no means a content farm.

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:49.890
In fact, they were and still are the exact
opposite only posting once maybe twice a month.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:54.870
Their goal with each video was to break down
the most complex topics you can think of in

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.810
the most elegant ways possible using beautiful
animations.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:04.689
Kurzgesagt was truly a passionate team of
illustrators, animators, writers, researchers,

00:09:04.689 --> 00:09:10.160
and music producers wanting to create the
absolute best content on the platform.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:13.600
And this showed, it shined right through.

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:20.020
Over the past 10 years, they’ve only posted
a 193 videos but almost all of these videos

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:22.350
have tens of millions of views each.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:26.769
Seeing this, it wasn't long until copycats
started entering the scene.

00:09:26.769 --> 00:09:30.510
Likely the most infamous of which being the
Infographics Show.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:35.410
I don’t wanna bag on the Infographics Show
too much as they do post some bangers from

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:40.350
time to time like how El Chapo escaped from
prison or how Ted Bundy was caught.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:45.339
Also, they’ve actually been around longer
than Kurzgesagt being founded in 2011.

00:09:45.339 --> 00:09:50.670
But, with that being said, they very much
operate like a content farm given that they

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:54.579
post 2 to 3 videos every single day.

00:09:54.579 --> 00:09:59.110
Also, their animations and sound design aren’t
unique and inspired with each upload like

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:00.110
Kurzgesagt.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:04.070
Rather, they just use a bunch of templates
and premade animations.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:09.180
But, the soullessness of the Infographics
Show was nothing in comparison to what we

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.690
saw on November 15, 2016.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:17.459
This was the fateful day that 5 minute crafts
was launched.

00:10:17.459 --> 00:10:21.769
If you’ve already erased this channel from
your brain, let me give you a warning as I’m

00:10:21.769 --> 00:10:25.460
about to give you a painful refresher of their
content.

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:32.140
Yeah, I think that’s all that needs to be
shown.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:36.930
Originally, I was gonna show more examples
but I don’t want to be responsible for causing

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:39.329
brain damage so we’ll leave it there.

00:10:39.329 --> 00:10:44.450
Seriously though, I don’t think you could
get a single person in the entire world to

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:51.389
argue that 5 Minute Crafts actually makes
good content but man are their videos so clickable.

00:10:51.389 --> 00:10:59.020
16 FUNNY HACKS THAT WORK MAGIC. 21 SIMPLE
LIFE HACKS TO LOOK STUNNING EVERY DAY. 5-Minute

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:03.060
Crafts compilation: This video is a treasure
trove!

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.230
This was their strategy.

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:09.100
They were never trying to make content that
moves us or something.

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:13.110
Their goal was really just to get us to click
in an era where clickbait on YouTube worked

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:14.130
extremely well.

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:19.560
A couple months after 5 minute crafts popped
up, we saw another such channel pop up: Bright

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:20.560
Side.

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:25.839
Bright Side was really just a whole lot more
of the same thing which is not that surprising

00:11:25.839 --> 00:11:30.459
when you consider that it’s owned by the
same parent company: The Soul Publishing.

00:11:30.459 --> 00:11:36.351
The Soul Publishing also owns 123 GO!, Slick
Slime Sam, 5 year crafts, La La life, Actually

00:11:36.351 --> 00:11:38.760
Happened and so many more.

00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:42.800
These guys are literally the definition of
a content farm.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.459
Across all their channels, they post like
50 videos every single day across 21 languages.

00:11:48.459 --> 00:11:52.350
And for the longest time, this was working
phenomenally.

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:58.520
In fact, the parent company is the largest
YouTube corporation on the planet with over

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.320
1.2 billion subscribers on YouTube alone.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.790
But, more recently, things aren’t looking
all that great.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:11.850
While 5 minute crafts has 80 million subscribers,
many of their recent upload don’t even cross

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:12.850
100,000 views.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:17.269
It’s even worse at Bright Side where many
of their uploads don’t even cross 10,000

00:12:17.269 --> 00:12:20.510
views despite having 44 million subscribers.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:25.660
The Infographics Show is fairing a lot better
but they too have fallen substantially from

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.500
their peak a couple years ago.

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:30.029
You know who hasn’t fallen off though?

00:12:30.029 --> 00:12:31.029
Kurzgesagt.

00:12:31.029 --> 00:12:36.399
To this day, they still put out the best videos
they can and they still get millions of views

00:12:36.399 --> 00:12:38.639
on each and every video.

00:12:38.639 --> 00:12:44.400
So, clearly, the demand for this content is
still stronger than ever but trying to mass

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:48.760
produce these videos and sensationalize them
didn’t work out for these copycats over

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:55.800
the long term but what if you added in the
help of AI?

00:12:55.800 --> 00:13:05.470
AI VIDEOS:
The release of ChatGPT definitely lowered

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.040
the barrier for YouTube automation substantially.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:13.340
Instead of paying a script writer a few hundred
dollars, you could just get AI to write a

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:14.839
script for free.

00:13:14.839 --> 00:13:20.550
But, it wasn’t the ease itself that has
led to an explosion in AI content.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:28.370
Rather, I believe the explosion can be attributed
to YouTube gurus.

00:13:28.370 --> 00:13:32.940
Since the beginning of this year, we’ve
seen a massive influx in the number YouTube

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:38.850
gurus selling the idea of getting rich by
creating an automated AI YouTube channel.

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:43.389
They claim that they were able to make $30,000
within 30 days or a million within a year

00:13:43.389 --> 00:13:46.060
and that you can too.

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:50.089
All you have to do is sign up for their “Free”
webinar.

00:13:50.089 --> 00:13:55.279
To be honest, these website look exactly like
one of those Tai Lopez webinars.

00:13:55.279 --> 00:13:58.380
They even have the timer for crying out loud.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:04.560
Now, let me say, there is some legitimacy
to this as there is with all get rich quick

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:05.560
schemes.

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:08.860
Is it possible to get rich with with an automated
YouTube channel.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:09.860
Sure.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:11.240
Is it probable?

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:12.610
Of course not.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:17.600
It’s just like dropshipping or Amazon FBA
or stock trading.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:21.459
Possible yes, probable no.

00:14:21.459 --> 00:14:26.980
And just like the Amazon FBA gurus led to
Amazon being flooded with garbage from Alibaba,

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:32.470
the YouTube automation gurus are leading to
YouTube being flooded with AI garbage.

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:36.850
At this point you can go to basically any
niche you want and you’ll find the same

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:48.620
videos with the classic robot voice, the sterile
scripts, and the collage of stock footage.

00:14:48.620 --> 00:14:53.529
Now, I’ve got nothing against these creators.

00:14:53.529 --> 00:14:55.610
If you’ve made this work, that’s awesome!

00:14:55.610 --> 00:15:01.230
But that’s not the type of content that
I want to be associated with nor is that the

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:03.860
direction that I want to see YouTube head
in.

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:08.850
And that’s why we’re talking face to face
here for the first time in 4 years.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:13.100
Also, none of this is to say that I’m against
creators using AI.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:17.839
AI is a phenomenal tool that absolutely should
be leveraged to make the entire production

00:15:17.839 --> 00:15:22.050
process more efficient and to put out the
best content possible.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:27.680
AI, however, should not be used to make low
quality spam to milk views.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:29.190
Drop a like if you agree.

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.589
Fortunately, I don’t think AI garbage is
here to stay.

00:15:31.589 --> 00:15:35.160
It’s a pretty new trend so I don’t expect
it to disappear immedietely.

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:40.990
In fact, it could very well gain momentum
over the next 2, 3, 4 years.

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:46.290
But zooming out to 5 or 10 years, I think
we’ll look back at this time period and

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:52.340
the AI spam the same way that we look back
on vlogs, pranks, top 10 channels, and Bright

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:53.410
Side.

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:58.139
The bottomline is that people come here for
authenticity and relatibility.

00:15:58.139 --> 00:16:00.329
When AI adds to that?

00:16:00.329 --> 00:16:01.329
Perfect.

00:16:01.329 --> 00:16:07.060
When AI takes away from that though, well,
I think that content can only go so far but

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:09.400
that’s just what I think.

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:12.370
I would also argue that there is a sponsorship
problem on YouTube.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:17.660
Check out this video to learn more.

